{"cuisine/hungarian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hungarian-cuisine","n":"Hungarian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Hungary","intro":[["Hungarian cuisine is the cuisine characteristic of the nation of Hungary, and its primary ethnic group, Hungarians. Hungarian cuisine has been described as being the spiciest cuisine in Europe. This can largely be attributed to the use of their piquant native spice, Hungarian paprika, in many of their dishes. A mild version of the spice, Hungarian sweet paprika, is commonly used as an alternative. Traditional Hungarian dishes are primarily based on meats, seasonal vegetables, fruits, bread, and dairy products."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hungarian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hungarian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":19,"links":[["Arany galuska","dish/arany-galuska"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chicken paprikash","dish/chicken-paprikash"],["Esterházy torte","dish/esterhazy-torte"],["Goulash","dish/goulash"],["Gulyásleves","dish/gulyasleves"],["Jókai bean soup","dish/jokai-bean-soup"],["Kürtőskalács","dish/kurtoskalacs"],["Lecsó","dish/lecso"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"],["Palóc soup","dish/paloc-soup"],["Pörkölt","dish/porkolt"],["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"],["Rigójancsi","dish/rigojancsi"],["Sour cherry soup","dish/sour-cherry-soup"],["Speckkuchen","dish/speckkuchen"],["Székelykáposzta","dish/szekelykaposzta"],["Tarhonya","dish/tarhonya"],["Trdelník","dish/trdelnik"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Zsuzsa Is in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com","note":"Hungarian-born home cook's blog of traditional Hungarian recipes.","url":"https://zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com/2010/01","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Cuisine of Hungary","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"George Lang, 1971","note":"Classic English-language history and recipe collection by the Hungarian-born restaurateur.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780517169636","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780517169636"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Agaricus","ingredient/agaricus"],["Allspice","ingredient/allspice"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/75/Hungarian_Guly%C3%A1s.jpg/500px-Hungarian_Guly%C3%A1s.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"m.louis","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Guly%C3%A1s.jpg"}},"cuisine/portuguese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/portuguese-cuisine","n":"Portuguese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Portugal","intro":[["Portuguese cuisine consists of the traditions and practices of cooking in Portugal. The oldest known book on Portuguese cuisine, entitled Livro de Cozinha da Infanta D. Maria de Portugal, from the 16th century, describes many popular dishes of meat, fish, poultry and others."],["Culinária Portuguesa, by António-Maria De Oliveira Bello, better known as Olleboma, was published in 1936."],["Despite being relatively restricted to an Atlantic,  Celtic sustenance, the Portuguese cuisine also has strong French and Mediterranean influences."],["The influence of Portugal's spice trade in the East Indies, Africa, and the Americas is also notable, especially in the wide variety of spices used. These spices include piri piri (small, fiery chili peppers), white pepper, black pepper, saffron, paprika, clove, allspice, cumin, cinnamon and nutmeg, used in meat, fish or multiple savoury dishes from continental Portugal, the Azores and Madeira islands."],["Cinnamon, vanilla, lemon zest, orange zest, aniseed, clove and allspice are used in many traditional desserts and some savoury dishes."],["Garlic and onions are widely used, as are herbs; bay leaf, parsley, oregano, thyme, mint, marjoram, rosemary and coriander are the most prevalent."],["Olive oil is one of the bases of Portuguese cuisine, which is used both for cooking and flavouring meals. This has led to a unique classification of olive oils in Portugal, depending on their acidity: 1.5 degrees is only for cooking with (virgin olive oil); anything lower than 1 degree is good for dousing over fish, potatoes and vegetables (extra virgin). 0.7, 0.5 or even 0.3 degrees are for those who do not enjoy the taste of olive oil, or who wish to use it in, for example, mayonnaise or a sauce where the taste is meant to be disguised."],["Portuguese dishes are based on the Atlantic diet and include meats, seafood (fish, crustaceans such as lobster, crab, shrimps, prawns, octopus, and molluscs such as scallops, clams and barnacles), numerous vegetable varieties (brassica family), legumes and desserts (cakes being the most numerous)."],["Portuguese often consume rice, potatoes, rapini (known as grelos), and bread with their meals, and there are numerous varieties of traditional fresh breads like broa, which may also have regional and national variations within the countries under Lusophone or Galician influence."],["In a wider sense, Portuguese and Galician cuisine share many traditions and features."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"portuguese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":31,"links":[["Alheira","dish/alheira"],["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"],["Arroz de marisco","dish/arroz-de-marisco"],["Bacalhau à Brás","dish/bacalhau-a-bras"],["Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá","dish/bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa"],["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"],["Bacalhau à Narcisa","dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa"],["Bacalhau à Zé do Pipo","dish/bacalhau-a-ze-do-pipo"],["Bacalhau com natas","dish/bacalhau-com-natas"],["Batatas a murro","dish/batatas-a-murro"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bolo de bolacha","dish/bolo-de-bolacha"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Encharcada","dish/encharcada"],["Fartura","dish/fartura"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"],["Fios de ovos","dish/fios-de-ovos"],["Francesinha","dish/francesinha"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Pão Alentejano","dish/pao-alentejano"],["Pão de Ló","dish/pao-de-lo"],["Pataniscas","dish/pataniscas"],["Polvo à lagareiro","dish/polvo-a-lagareiro"],["Prego (Portuguese sandwich)","dish/prego-portuguese-sandwich"],["Pudim Abade de Priscos","dish/pudim-abade-de-priscos"],["Pudim Molotov","dish/pudim-molotov"],["Sericaia","dish/sericaia"],["Serradura","dish/serradura"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The New Portuguese Table","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"David Leite, 2009","note":"Portuguese-American writer's take on traditional and modern Portuguese cooking.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/99720/the-new-portuguese-table-by-david-leite-photography-by-nuno-correia/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780307394415"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Fried potatoes","ingredient/fried-potatoes"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg yolks","ingredient/egg-yolks"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Onions","ingredient/onions"],["Grelos","ingredient/grelos"],["Olives","ingredient/olives"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Marie biscuit","ingredient/marie-biscuit"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/c2/Grilled_Sardines_5.50%E2%82%AC_Marisqueira_O_Varino_Nazar%C3%A9_%283785526688%29.jpg/500px-Grilled_Sardines_5.50%E2%82%AC_Marisqueira_O_Varino_Nazar%C3%A9_%283785526688%29.jpg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"Yusuke Kawasaki from Tokyo, Japan","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grilled_Sardines_5.50%E2%82%AC_Marisqueira_O_Varino_Nazar%C3%A9_(3785526688).jpg"}},"dish/bah-uan":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bah-uan","n":"Bah-uân","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Taiwan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bah-uân is a Taiwanese dumpling made of starch and rice flour, filled with meat and vegetables. It is gelatinous and translucent, and often considered street food."]],"src":"wikipedia